## 1. What Active Binary Defense is

Active Binary Defense is openOODA's built-in, secure-by-default software protection architecture.
Unlike traditional systems where security is an afterthought or requires external tools, openOODA binaries are natively fortified at compile time and actively self-defending at runtime.

By default, every compilation generates a morphologically diverse binary that resists reverse engineering, neutralizes exploit chains, traps unauthorized probing, and heals in-memory modifications without runtime penalty.

## 2. The 5 Core Defense Categories

### 2.1 Moving Target Defense (MTD) & Metamorphic Transmutation
- **Control Flow Flattening**: Breaks hierarchical `if`/`while` structures into Chenxi Wang state-machine switch dispatchers, destroying high-level algorithmic decompilation in IDA Pro and Ghidra.
- **Basic Block Permutation**: Randomizes the physical layout of basic blocks across memory on every build using cryptographic entropy seeds, inserting unconditional branch fixups automatically.
- **Number-Theoretic Opaque Predicates**: Injects algebraic invariants (`(x*(x+1)) % 2 == 0`) and quadratic non-residues mod 7 (`(7*y^2 - 1) % 7 == (x^2) % 7` is false), forcing exponential path explosion in symbolic execution engines (angr, Z3).
- **Instruction Substitution**: Replaces standard idioms (`x = 0`) with randomized algebraic equivalents (`xor x, x`, `sub x, x`).

### 2.2 Exploit Mitigation & Anti-ROP/JOP Defenses
- **Gadget Elimination**: Scans intermediate bytecode for unintended `0xC3` (`ret`) or `0xFF 0x25` (`jmp`) byte alignments across instruction boundaries and injects safe multi-byte NOP padders.
- **Compile-Time String Encryption**: Encrypts string literals with dynamic rolling XOR keys and deploys stack-transient decrypters that scrub plaintext from memory immediately after consumption.

### 2.3 Cyber Deception & Binary Honeynets
- **Decoy Entry Points**: Injects capability-starved honeypot routines (`admin_override`, `debug_backdoor`, `crypto_key_dump`) into the symbol table.
- **Canary Breadcrumbs**: Plants fake credentials (`sk_live_canary_*`) and canary pointers. Probing a decoy triggers instant fail-closed adversary isolation and alerts.

### 2.4 Runtime Application Self-Protection (RASP) & Autonomic Self-Healing
- **In-Memory Watchdog**: Registers authenticated SHA256 checksums of executable `.text` pages during startup.
- **Live Memory Rollback**: If an unauthorized debugger or malware injects an inline hook (`0xCC`, `0xE9`), the runtime detects the divergence and automatically rolls back pristine gold-master bytes live in RAM without crashing the process.

### 2.5 Object-Capability (OCap) & Zero-Trust Security
- **Zero Ambient Authority**: Morphed code, dispatchers, and honeypots cannot synthesize capability tokens (`&FsReadCap`, `&NetCap`, `&ProcessCap`).
- **Cryptographic Token Sealing**: Runtime tokens are 64-bit entropy-seeded values validated with process-local HMAC keys.

## 3. Opt-Out (Secure-by-Default) Model

Binary defense is **ON by default**. You do not need compiler flags to harden your release binary.

| Build Command | Defense Posture | Target Use Case |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `oodac emit-c main.oo` | **Fortified by Default** (MTD, ROP defense, encrypted strings, honeynets) | Production Release |
| `oodac emit-c --plain main.oo` | **Opt-Out (Debug)** (Clean, un-morphed C, linear CFGs) | GDB / LLDB Stepping |
| `ooda run --debug main.oo` | **Opt-Out (Debug)** (Disabled self-healing for breakpoints) | Local Profiling |

